---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/code-block.astro';
import GuidePage from '../../layouts/guide-page.astro';
---

<GuidePage title="Theming" lead="Two themes, two densities and a few open tokens. The laws stay fixed.">
  <h2 id="theme">Ink and Paper</h2>
  <p>
    With no choice made, the theme follows the system. <code>data-theme="dark"</code> or <code>"light"</code> on
    <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, or on any region, forces it; a dark code sample can sit in a light article.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="index.html" code={`<html data-theme="dark" data-density="compact">`} />

  <h2 id="density">Density</h2>
  <p>
    <code>comfortable</code> (the default) or <code>compact</code>, on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> or any region: control
    heights, cell padding and type steps follow it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open">The open tokens</h2>
  <p>
    The accent, the three free hues (blue, cyan, magenta), the font families (as long as a mono and a sans stay paired)
    and density are yours to set. Set colours as <code>light-dark()</code> pairs in your theme file:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label="nib-theme.css"
    code={`:root {
  --nib-accent: light-dark(#02806c, #00967d);
  --nib-accent-text: light-dark(#007561, #009e84);
}`}
  />
  <p>
    Then run <code>nib theme check</code>: it proofs contrast on all three surfaces in both themes, colour-vision
    distance and collisions with the fixed hues (<a href="/guides/cli/">CLI</a>). The
    <a href="/play/">proof press</a> does the same live, and exports the stylesheet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="presets">Presets</h2>
  <p>
    A preset bundles page defaults, layout recipes and a hue mapping for one kind of site: docs, dashboard, blog or
    landing. See <a href="/components/presets/">Presets</a>.
  </p>
</GuidePage>
